#!/bin/sh
cat <<'TEXT'
incidentlab toolbox: the Docker VM from the inside (host PID namespace, privileged, cgroups read-only).
Run `kernel-check` first: it says which kernel tools work on this VM.

The 60-second checklist (Ctrl-C stops the ones that repeat)
  uptime                 load average over 1, 5, 15 min
  dmesg -T | tail        kernel errors: OOM kills, dropped packets
  vmstat 1               r against nproc, si/so, us sy id wa st
  mpstat -P ALL 1        one CPU busy while the others idle?
  pidstat 1              %CPU per process
  iostat -xz 1           r_await, w_await, aqu-sz, %util per disk
  free -m                available (not free)
  sar -n DEV 1           rxkB/s, txkB/s per interface
  sar -n TCP,ETCP 1      active/s, passive/s, retrans/s
  top                    press 1 for every CPU, H for threads, q to quit

Find a service's process
  pgrep -f checkout-api      api          pgrep -f pricing-api   pricing
  pgrep -f orders-worker     worker       pgrep -o -x postgres   Postgres (oldest postgres process)
  containers                 every container with its PID and cgroup

Read a container's cgroup
  C=$(cg $(pgrep -f checkout-api))
  cat $C/cpu.max $C/memory.max           the limits
  cat $C/cpu.stat                        nr_periods, nr_throttled, throttled_usec
  cat $C/memory.current $C/memory.events usage now, oom_kill
  cat $C/cpu.pressure                    time spent waiting for a CPU (PSI)

Enter a container's network namespace (the tools stay the toolbox's)
  nsenter -t $(pgrep -f checkout-api) -n ss -tn
  nsenter -t $(pgrep -f checkout-api) -n sar -n TCP,ETCP 1
  nsenter -t 1 -n sar -n DEV 1           the VM's own interfaces

Ask the services
  psql                                   same as PGPASSWORD=lab psql -h postgres -U lab lab
  curl -s http://api:8000/metrics | grep ^db_pool
  curl -s http://worker:8002/metrics | grep ^kafka_consumer_lag

Kernel tools: they need BPF, perf events and tracefs from the VM kernel. They may fail on Docker Desktop.
  bpftrace -e 'profile:hz:99 { @[comm] = count(); } interval:s:10 { exit(); }'
  perf top -e cpu-clock

`toolbox-help` prints this again. docs/toolbox.md explains every command.
TEXT
